Fit one drag multiplier per launch loft and keep it constant throughout that run. Each truth case needs a label, launch loft, time vector, and N×3 ECI position and velocity histories. The first position and velocity initialize your propagator. The remaining samples tell the fitter how well it did. You don’t need an angle-of-attack history for this study.

The two files are available individually for transcription, or together as a ZIP.

fit_loft_drag.m⁠￼ · drag_study_scale.m⁠￼ · Loft_Drag_Study.zip⁠￼

1. Change one line in Drag.m

Replace inverseBeta = model.K_norm * CD; with:

inverseBeta = drag_study_scale() * model.K_norm * CD;

Put both downloaded functions alongside your propagator. drag_study_scale() defaults to 1. During a trial, the fitter sets it once, so the predictor and corrector both use the same multiplier. Your five-input call to propagate_traj works as before. The CD map still reports the aerodynamic table coefficient, and the multiplier acts on the resulting drag acceleration.

2. Enter your cases and run the study

makeRun = @(label,loft,t,R,V) struct('label',label,'loftDeg',loft, ...
                                     'time',t(:),'R',R,'V',V);
runs(1) = makeRun('Case A', 10, tA, RA, VA);
runs(2) = makeRun('Case B', 15, tB, RB, VB);
runs(3) = makeRun('Case C', 25, tC, RC, VC);
% Add one line per additional trajectory.
scaleGrid = 0.90:0.025:1.10;
out = fit_loft_drag(runs, dt, scaleGrid, 100, 1, 15);
out.fit
out.globalScale
out.cases

Use your normal propagation step for dt. The example weights, 100 metres and 1 m/s, mean that a 100 m position error contributes as much to the score as a 1 m/s velocity error. The fitter interpolates the propagated states onto the truth timestamps and averages the squared errors over time. Runs at the same loft share a fitted multiplier.

The final 15 holds out every 15° case. In this example, the study fits the 10° and 25° cases, linearly interpolates their multipliers to predict the 15° scale, then runs the 15° trajectory to score that prediction. For your low-loft question, hold out a low loft with a training loft on either side. Omit the last argument to fit all cases.

3. Decide whether loft dependence helps

Result	What it tells you
out.fit	Best multiplier at each training loft. A value of 0.98 reduces drag acceleration by 2% at the same state.
out.globalScale	Best single multiplier across all training runs, giving each run equal weight.
out.cases	For each run, Jbase scores the original model, Jglobal scores the single multiplier, and Jloft scores the loft-dependent multiplier. Lower is better.

Compare Jloft against Jglobal on the held-out low-loft rows. That answers whether knowing loft actually helps predict a trajectory excluded from fitting. Since lift is omitted, a successful multiplier is an empirical correction for this version of the model. Its value can change when you add lift.

For the optimizer, I used a coarse sweep followed by fminbnd around the best sampled scale. That gives you a bounded, one-parameter fit in base MATLAB and works before R2021b. plot(out.grid,out.sweep.') shows the score versus multiplier for each training loft. A flat curve means the trajectory gives you little basis for choosing a precise multiplier. A narrow minimum can fall between grid points. 

An independent Python check on synthetic histories recovered a held-out multiplier of 0.916002 against a known 0.916000. I haven’t run the MATLAB files with your propagator or truth data. Use your working Reynolds-number routine when you run them, since the shared Drag.m template still contains the ReynoldsNotConfigured placeholder.
